<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="41872" href="schedule/2016/fall/CWL/119/41872.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2016" href="schedule/2016.xml">2016</calendarYear><term id="120168" href="schedule/2016/fall.xml">Fall 2016</term><subject id="CWL" href="schedule/2016/fall/CWL.xml">Comparative and World Literature</subject><course id="119" href="schedule/2016/fall/CWL/119.xml">Literature of Fantasy</course></parents><sectionNumber>C  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Introduction to the rich traditions of fantasy writing in world literature. While the commercial category of fantasy post-Tolkien will often be the focal point, individual instructors may choose to focus on alternate definitions of the genre: literatures of the fantastic, the uncanny, and the weird; fantasy before the Enlightenment and the advent of realism; fantasy for young adult or child readers; and so on.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2016-08-22Z</startDate><endDate>2016-12-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>10:00 AM</start><end>10:50 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MWF    </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>104</roomNumber><buildingName>English Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Hartley-Kroeger" firstName="F">Hartley-Kroeger, F</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>